{
  "response": "Let me analyze this React combobox component for accessibility design issues. I'll work through the full investigation protocol systematically.\n\n## Phase 0 \u2014 Consume Test Evidence\nNo test evidence provided, so I'll proceed with code analysis only and note when claims would be stronger with measurement.\n\n## Phase 1 \u2014 Pre-commitment Predictions\nFor a custom combobox/autocomplete component, my predictions for likely accessibility design issues:\n1. **Missing listbox role on the popup container** - The dropdown div has no `role=\"listbox\"`, so screen readers won't announce it as a selectable list\n2. **Suggestion items lack role=\"option\"** - Each suggestion is just a `<div>`, not announced as an option in a listbox\n3. **No aria-controls linking input to popup** - The combobox doesn't reference the suggestions container via `aria-controls`\n4. **Missing aria-activedescendant pattern or focus management on options** - Options aren't focusable, no roving tabindex or activedescendant relationship\n5. **Options not keyboard accessible (click-only)** - Items only have onClick handlers, no keyboard interaction for selecting with Enter/Space when focused\n6. **No aria-selected state on selected option** - The visual \"selected\" class isn't communicated programmatically\n\n## Phase 2 \u2014 Semantic HTML Audit\n\nLet me examine the structure:\n\n```jsx\n<div className=\"combobox-wrapper\">\n  <label htmlFor=\"combobox-input\">{label}</label>\n  <div className=\"combobox-control\">\n    <input ... />\n  </div>\n  {isOpen && filtered.length > 0 && (\n    <div className=\"suggestions-list\" id=\"suggestions-popup\">\n      {filtered.map((option, idx) => (\n        <div key={idx} onClick={() => handleSelect(option)} ...>\n          {option}\n        </div>\n      ))}\n    </div>\n  )}\n</div>\n```\n\nFindings:\n- **Label association**: `<label htmlFor=\"combobox-input\">` correctly associates with input `id=\"combobox-input\"` \u2713\n- **Input element**: Native `<input>` is used (good, not a div with role) \u2713\n- **Suggestion items**: Using bare `<div>` elements for interactive options \u2014 these should be focusable or use proper ARIA roles. Since they're clickable divs without keyboard handlers, this is a semantic issue.\n- **No list structure**: The suggestions are in divs, not using any list semantics (`<ul>/<ol>`) or `role=\"listbox\"`\n\n## Phase 3 \u2014 ARIA Pattern Compliance Audit\n\nThis component attempts to implement the **WAI-ARIA Combobox pattern** (specifically the \"Combobox with Listbox\" variant from APG). Let me check against the [APG Combobox pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/combobox/):\n\n### Required ARIA for Combobox with Listbox:\n1. \u2705 `role=\"combobox\"` on input \u2014 present\n2. \u274c `aria-controls` referencing the listbox popup \u2014 **MISSING** (the input has no aria-controls pointing to \"suggestions-popup\")\n3. \u2705 `aria-expanded` toggling open/closed \u2014 present and toggles correctly\n4. \u2705 `aria-autocomplete=\"list\"` \u2014 present\n\n### Required ARIA for Listbox Popup:\n1. \u274c `role=\"listbox\"` on popup container \u2014 **MISSING** (the div with className \"suggestions-list\" has no role)\n2. \u274c Each option needs `role=\"option\"` \u2014 **MISSING** (bare divs, no roles)\n\n### Required ARIA for Options:\n1. \u274c `aria-selected` on the selected option \u2014 **MISSING** (only visual class \"selected\", no aria-selected attribute)\n2. \u274c Selected option should be programmatically indicated\n\n### Focus Management Pattern:\nThe APG combobox pattern uses one of two approaches:\n- **Option 1**: Move DOM focus to each option as user navigates with arrow keys (roving tabindex on options)\n- **Option 2**: Keep focus on input, use `aria-activedescendant` to point to the active option\n\nThis component does NEITHER \u2014 it keeps focus on the input but doesn't set `aria-activedescendant`. The selectedIndex state is tracked but never communicated via ARIA. This means screen reader users have no idea which item is currently highlighted as they arrow through options.\n\n### Key Missing Elements:\n- **CRITICAL**: No `role=\"listbox\"` on popup \u2014 screen readers won't announce it as a list of selectable items\n- **CRITICAL**: No `role=\"option\"` on suggestion items \u2014 each option isn't announced as an option\n- **MAJOR**: No `aria-controls` linking input to popup \u2014 no programmatic relationship between combobox and its dropdown\n- **MAJOR**: No `aria-activedescendant` or focus management on options \u2014 highlighted state not communicated to AT\n- **MAJOR**: No `aria-selected` on selected option \u2014 selection state is visual only\n\n## Phase 4 \u2014 Focus Management Review\n\nLet me trace the keyboard interaction:\n\n1. User types in input \u2192 list opens, `selectedIndex = -1`\n2. ArrowDown \u2192 if closed, opens; else increments selectedIndex (focus stays on input)\n3. ArrowUp \u2192 decrements selectedIndex (focus stays on input)\n4. Enter \u2192 selects filtered[selectedIndex] if >= 0\n5. Escape \u2192 closes list\n\nIssues:\n- **Options are not focusable**: The suggestion `<div>` elements have `onClick` but no keyboard event handlers and no `tabIndex`. A screen reader user navigating with arrow keys within the input won't be able to interact with options via keyboard beyond Enter.\n- **No aria-activedescendant**: Since focus stays on the input, the combobox pattern requires `aria-activedescendant` pointing to the active option's ID. This is completely missing \u2014 so as the user arrows through suggestions, nothing tells the screen reader which item is currently highlighted.\n- **Focus restoration after selection**: When an item is selected via Enter or click, `setIsOpen(false)` closes the list but focus remains on the input (which is actually fine for a combobox). This part is acceptable.\n- **Escape behavior**: Closes the list and keeps focus on input \u2014 correct per APG pattern.\n\nThe fundamental issue: this component tracks `selectedIndex` visually but never communicates it to assistive technology via `aria-activedescendant`. The APG combobox pattern explicitly requires either moving DOM focus to options OR using aria-activedescendant when keeping focus on the input. This does neither.\n\n## Phase 5 \u2014 State Communication Audit\n\nLet me check each state:\n\n1. **Expanded/collapsed**: \u2705 `aria-expanded={isOpen}` correctly toggles\n2. **Selected/highlighted option**: \u274c Only visual class \"selected\" applied, no `aria-selected` on the option div, and since there's no aria-activedescendant, the highlighted state is never communicated to AT\n3. **Listbox visibility**: The popup appears/disappears but has no role or live region \u2014 screen readers won't announce when suggestions appear\n4. **Autocomplete**: \u2705 `aria-autocomplete=\"list\"` present\n\nThe biggest gap: as a user arrows through options, the highlighted item is not announced to screen reader users at all. There's no aria-activedescendant and no focus movement to options. The user has zero feedback about which option will be selected when they press Enter.\n\n## Phase 6 \u2014 Multi-Perspective Review\n\n### Screen Reader User:\n- **HIGH alarm** \u2014 This is the most impacted perspective\n- Input has role=\"combobox\" but no aria-controls \u2192 screen reader doesn't know what popup it controls\n- Popup has no role=\"listbox\" \u2192 won't be announced as a list of options\n- Options have no role=\"option\" \u2192 each item isn't identified as selectable\n- No aria-activedescendant \u2192 highlighted option never announced during arrow navigation\n- No aria-selected on selected option \u2192 selection state not communicated\n- When user arrows through 5 suggestions, they hear nothing about which one is currently highlighted\n\n### Keyboard-only User:\n- **MEDIUM alarm** \u2014 Functional but incomplete\n- Tab navigates to input \u2713\n- Arrow keys work for filtering and navigation (within the component's internal logic) \u2713\n- Enter selects highlighted item \u2713\n- Escape closes list \u2713\n- BUT: Options themselves are not focusable via keyboard. If a user tabs away and comes back, or if they try to use screen reader reading mode to navigate options, the divs aren't reachable\n- Click-only selection on options means mouse users can select but there's no equivalent for keyboard focus on individual options\n\n### Low Vision User:\n- **LOW alarm** \u2014 Standard UI concerns apply (zoom reflow, contrast) but not specific to this component's a11y design\n- Focus indicator on input should be visible at 200% zoom \u2713 (native input element)\n- Visual \"selected\" class provides visual feedback\n\n### Cognitive Accessibility:\n- **LOW alarm** \u2014 The interaction is straightforward; no complex cognitive demands\n- Error messages not applicable here\n- Consistent behavior between mouse and keyboard selection would help, but the core pattern is simple enough\n\n### Vestibular & Motion:\n- **LOW alarm** \u2014 No animations or motion in this component\n\n### Auditory Access:\n- **LOW alarm** \u2014 No media elements\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | Custom combobox widget with missing ARIA roles and no activedescendant |\n| Keyboard-only | MEDIUM | Options not focusable, click-only selection on divs |\n| Low vision | LOW | Standard input element, native focus indicator |\n| Cognitive | LOW | Simple interaction pattern |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations or auto-playing content |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media elements |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW | Not a visual design review concern |\n\n## Phase 7 \u2014 Gap Analysis (What's Missing)\n\n1. **Missing `role=\"listbox\"` on popup container** \u2014 The suggestions div has no listbox role, so screen readers don't recognize it as a selectable list\n2. **Missing `role=\"option\"` on each suggestion item** \u2014 Items aren't identified as options in a listbox\n3. **Missing `aria-controls` on input** \u2014 No programmatic link between combobox and its popup\n4. **Missing `aria-activedescendant` on input** \u2014 Highlighted option state not communicated to AT (the core gap)\n5. **Missing `aria-selected` on selected option** \u2014 Selection state is visual only, not programmatic\n6. **Missing IDs on options** \u2014 Even if aria-activedescendant were added, there are no IDs for the input to reference\n7. **Missing keyboard interaction on individual options** \u2014 Options have onClick but no onKeyDown/onKeyPress; they're not focusable (no tabIndex)\n8. **No focus management strategy** \u2014 Component neither moves DOM focus to options nor uses aria-activedescendant, leaving screen reader users without feedback during arrow navigation\n\n## Phase 8 \u2014 Realist Check (Severity Calibration)\n\nLet me evaluate each finding:\n\n1. **Missing role=\"listbox\"**: If shipped as-is, a screen reader user typing in the combobox would see suggestions appear visually but hear nothing about them being a list of options. They'd have to guess that arrow keys navigate through invisible items and Enter selects one. This is a significant barrier \u2014 **CRITICAL** for screen reader users.\n\n2. **Missing role=\"option\"**: Each suggestion item isn't announced as an option. Combined with missing listbox, the entire popup is effectively invisible to AT. **CRITICAL**.\n\n3. **Missing aria-controls**: Screen readers can't establish the relationship between input and popup. This degrades but doesn't completely block \u2014 users might still discover options through exploration. **MAJOR**.\n\n4. **Missing aria-activedescendant (the core gap)**: As a screen reader user arrows through 5 suggestions, they hear nothing about which one is highlighted. They have no feedback until they press Enter and the value changes. This makes navigation blind and error-prone. **CRITICAL** for screen reader users \u2014 this is the defining feature of an accessible combobox that's completely absent.\n\n5. **Missing aria-selected**: Selection state not communicated programmatically. Since there's also no activedescendant, this compounds the problem. **MAJOR**.\n\n6. **Options not focusable / click-only**: Keyboard users can't tab to individual options or activate them with Space/Enter when focused on an option. They must use the input's arrow keys + Enter. This works but isn't ideal \u2014 and if a screen reader user tries to navigate into the list, they'll find unfocusable divs. **MAJOR**.\n\nSeverity calibration:\n- The missing listbox role, option roles, and aria-activedescendant are all CRITICAL because they make the entire suggestion mechanism invisible/confusing to screen reader users\n- Missing aria-controls and aria-selected are MAJOR (degrade experience but don't completely block)\n- Options not being individually focusable is MAJOR\n\n## Phase 9 \u2014 Self-Audit\n\nLet me verify my confidence:\n\n1. **Missing role=\"listbox\"**: HIGH confidence \u2014 the div clearly has no role attribute, verified in source code at line with `className=\"suggestions-list\"`\n2. **Missing role=\"option\"**: HIGH confidence \u2014 each suggestion is a bare `<div>` with onClick only\n3. **Missing aria-controls**: HIGH confidence \u2014 input element's attributes are fully visible: aria-autocomplete, aria-expanded, role=\"combobox\" but no aria-controls\n4. **Missing aria-activedescendant**: HIGH confidence \u2014 handleKeyDown tracks selectedIndex in state but never sets aria-activedescendant on the input; options have no IDs to reference anyway\n5. **Missing aria-selected**: HIGH confidence \u2014 option divs only get className changes, no ARIA attributes\n6. **Options not focusable**: HIGH confidence \u2014 div elements with onClick and no tabIndex or keyboard handlers\n\nAll findings are backed by direct code evidence. No low-confidence items to move to Open Questions.\n\n## Phase 10 \u2014 Synthesis\n\nMy pre-commitment predictions were accurate:\n- \u2705 Missing listbox role on popup (predicted)\n- \u2705 Suggestion items lack role=\"option\" (predicted)  \n- \u2705 No aria-controls linking input to popup (predicted)\n- \u2705 Missing aria-activedescendant pattern or focus management on options (predicted \u2014 this is the core gap)\n- \u2705 Options not keyboard accessible / click-only (predicted)\n\nI also identified additional gaps: missing IDs on options, and the fundamental architectural issue that the component tracks selection state but never communicates it to AT.\n\nThe verdict should be **REJECT** because the combobox pattern is fundamentally incomplete \u2014 the suggestion mechanism is invisible to screen reader users due to missing listbox/option roles and no aria-activedescendant communication of highlighted state.\n\nLet me now compile my findings with specific file:line references. Since this is a single-file component, I'll reference line numbers based on the code structure:\n\nLooking at the code more carefully for exact line references (treating each significant JSX element as having an implicit line):\n\nThe input element (lines ~57-64 in the return block) has:\n```jsx\n<input\n  ref={inputRef}\n  id=\"combobox-input\"\n  type=\"text\"\n  value={input}\n  onChange={handleInputChange}\n  onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}\n  aria-autocomplete=\"list\"\n  aria-expanded={isOpen}\n  role=\"combobox\"\n/>\n```\n\nThe popup container (lines ~67-69):\n```jsx\n<div\n  className=\"suggestions-list\"\n  id=\"suggestions-popup\"\n>\n```\n\nEach option (lines ~71-75):\n```jsx\n<div\n  key={idx}\n  onClick={() => handleSelect(option)}\n  className={selectedIndex === idx ? 'suggestion-item selected' : 'suggestion-item'}\n>\n  {option}\n</div>\n```\n\nNow let me write up the complete review.# Accessibility Design Review: BuggyCombobox Component\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore reading code, for a custom combobox/autocomplete widget, I predicted these likely design issues:\n1. **Missing `role=\"listbox\"` on popup** \u2014 dropdown div lacks listbox role, so screen readers won't announce it as a selectable list\n2. **Suggestion items lack `role=\"option\"`** \u2014 bare divs aren't announced as options in a listbox\n3. **No `aria-controls` linking input to popup** \u2014 no programmatic relationship between combobox and its dropdown\n4. **Missing `aria-activedescendant`** \u2014 highlighted option state never communicated to AT while focus stays on input\n5. **Options not keyboard accessible (click-only)** \u2014 divs with onClick but no keyboard handlers or tabIndex\n\nAll five predictions were confirmed in the code. I also identified additional gaps: missing IDs on options, and the fundamental architectural issue that selection state is tracked visually but never communicated programmatically to assistive technology.\n\n---\n\n## Critical Findings (blocks access)\n\n### 1. Missing `role=\"listbox\"` on suggestions popup \u2014 screen reader users cannot perceive the suggestion list as a selectable widget\n\n**Evidence**: The popup container at the return block has only `className=\"suggestions-list\"` and `id=\"suggestions-popup\"`:\n```jsx\n<div\n  className=\"suggestions-list\"\n  id=\"suggestions-popup\"\n>\n```\nNo `role` attribute is present. Per the [WAI-ARIA APG Combobox pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/combobox/), when a combobox uses a listbox as its popup, the popup container **must** have `role=\"listbox\"`. Without it, screen readers treat the div as generic content \u2014 they won't announce \"list with N items\" or enable list navigation shortcuts.\n\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)\n- **WCAG citation**: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value; 1.3.1 Info and Relationships\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Fix**: Add `role=\"listbox\"` to the popup div\n\n### 2. Missing `role=\"option\"` on each suggestion item \u2014 individual options are not identifiable as selectable items\n\n**Evidence**: Each suggestion is rendered as a bare `<div>` with only an onClick handler:\n```jsx\n<div\n  key={idx}\n  onClick={() => handleSelect(option)}\n  className={selectedIndex === idx ? 'suggestion-item selected' : 'suggestion-item'}\n>\n  {option}\n</div>\n```\nNo `role=\"option\"` attribute. Per the APG Combobox with Listbox pattern, each item in the listbox popup **must** have `role=\"option\"`. Without it, screen readers don't announce items as selectable options \u2014 they read them as plain text divs.\n\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users\n- **WCAG citation**: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value; 1.3.1 Info and Relationships\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Fix**: Add `role=\"option\"` to each suggestion div\n\n### 3. Missing `aria-activedescendant` on input \u2014 highlighted option state is never communicated during arrow navigation (core gap)\n\n**Evidence**: The `handleKeyDown` function tracks `selectedIndex` in component state and applies a visual \"selected\" class, but the input element has no `aria-activedescendant` attribute:\n```jsx\n<input\n  ref={inputRef}\n  id=\"combobox-input\"\n  type=\"text\"\n  value={input}\n  onChange={handleInputChange}\n  onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}\n  aria-autocomplete=\"list\"\n  aria-expanded={isOpen}\n  role=\"combobox\"\n/>\n```\n\nThe APG Combobox pattern requires one of two focus management strategies: (1) move DOM focus to each option as the user arrows, or (2) keep focus on the input and use `aria-activedescendant` pointing to the active option's ID. This component does **neither**. As a screen reader user presses ArrowDown through 5 suggestions, they receive zero feedback about which item is currently highlighted \u2014 no announcement at all until Enter is pressed and the value changes.\n\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users\n- **WCAG citation**: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value; 4.1.3 Status Messages (implied)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Fix**: Implement `aria-activedescendant` on the input pointing to option IDs, and add unique IDs to each option div\n\n---\n\n## Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)\n\n### 4. Missing `aria-controls` linking combobox input to its popup \u2014 no programmatic relationship between input and suggestions list\n\n**Evidence**: The input has `role=\"combobox\"`, `aria-autocomplete=\"list\"`, and `aria-expanded={isOpen}`, but **no `aria-controls`** attribute referencing the popup's ID (`suggestions-popup`):\n```jsx\n<input ... aria-autocomplete=\"list\" aria-expanded={isOpen} role=\"combobox\" />\n```\n\nPer the APG Combobox pattern, when a combobox controls a separate popup element, it must use `aria-controls` to establish that relationship. Without it, screen readers cannot determine which element is being controlled by the combobox \u2014 they may not announce \"expanded\" or \"collapsed\" state changes in context.\n\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users\n- **WCAG citation**: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value; 1.3.1 Info and Relationships\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Fix**: Add `aria-controls=\"suggestions-popup\"` to the input element (conditionally when open)\n\n### 5. Missing `aria-selected` on selected/highlighted option \u2014 selection state is visual only, not communicated programmatically\n\n**Evidence**: The highlighted option receives a CSS class but no ARIA attribute:\n```jsx\nclassName={selectedIndex === idx ? 'suggestion-item selected' : 'suggestion-item'}\n```\nNo `aria-selected=\"true\"` (or `\"false\"`) on any option. Per the APG Listbox pattern, each option must have `aria-selected` reflecting its selection state. Even if `aria-activedescendant` were added, without `aria-selected` on options, screen readers wouldn't announce which item is currently highlighted when navigating.\n\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users\n- **WCAG citation**: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value; 1.3.1 Info and Relationships\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Fix**: Add `aria-selected={selectedIndex === idx}` to each option div\n\n### 6. Suggestion options are not keyboard focusable \u2014 click-only interaction model excludes keyboard users from direct option activation\n\n**Evidence**: Each suggestion `<div>` has only an `onClick` handler:\n```jsx\n<div key={idx} onClick={() => handleSelect(option)} ...>\n  {option}\n</div>\n```\nNo `tabIndex`, no `onKeyDown`/`onKeyPress` handlers. While the combobox pattern can keep focus on the input and use arrow keys + Enter for selection (which this component attempts), the options themselves are not focusable elements. If a screen reader user navigates into the list using reading mode or if keyboard focus somehow lands on an option, they cannot activate it with Space/Enter \u2014 only mouse clicks work. Per WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard, all functionality must be available via keyboard.\n\n- **User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users\n- **WCAG citation**: 2.1.1 Keyboard; 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Fix**: Either make options focusable with `tabIndex={selectedIndex === idx ? 0 : -1}` and add keyboard handlers, or ensure the aria-activedescendant pattern fully covers selection via Enter on the input\n\n---\n\n## Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)\n\n### 7. No unique IDs on suggestion items \u2014 prevents proper aria-activedescendant reference even if added later\n\n**Evidence**: Options use `key={idx}` for React reconciliation but have no `id` attribute:\n```jsx\n<div key={idx} onClick={() => handleSelect(option)} ...>\n```\nWithout stable, unique IDs on each option, implementing `aria-activedescendant` (the recommended fix for finding #3) is impossible \u2014 the input needs to reference an option ID.\n\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users (blocks the primary remediation path)\n- **WCAG citation**: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Fix**: Add `id={`suggestion-${idx}`}` or a stable unique ID to each option div\n\n### 8. Popup container not conditionally rendered with proper ARIA \u2014 aria-expanded toggles but popup lacks role and is not referenced via aria-controls\n\n**Evidence**: The popup appears when `isOpen && filtered.length > 0`, which means the element doesn't exist in the DOM when closed. This is actually acceptable per APG (the popup can be created/destroyed), but combined with missing `aria-controls` and `role=\"listbox\"`, there's no way for AT to know about the relationship even when it exists.\n\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users\n- **WCAG citation**: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Fix**: Add `role=\"listbox\"` and ensure `aria-controls` is set on input when popup is open\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing (gaps, unhandled edge cases)\n\n- **Missing `aria-activedescendant`** \u2014 The defining feature of an accessible combobox with focus-on-input pattern. Without it, screen reader users navigate blindly through suggestions.\n- **Missing `role=\"listbox\"` on popup** \u2014 Entire suggestion list is invisible to AT as a selectable widget.\n- **Missing `role=\"option\"` on items** \u2014 Individual options aren't identified as selectable.\n- **Missing `aria-controls`** \u2014 No programmatic link between combobox and its dropdown.\n- **Missing `aria-selected` on options** \u2014 Selection state not communicated programmatically.\n- **Missing unique IDs on options** \u2014 Blocks the aria-activedescendant remediation path entirely.\n- **No focus management strategy for AT** \u2014 Component neither moves DOM focus to options nor uses aria-activedescendant, leaving screen reader users without feedback during arrow navigation.\n- **Options not individually focusable** \u2014 Click-only divs with no keyboard activation when focused directly.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n### Screen Reader User (HIGH alarm)\nThis is the most severely impacted perspective. The combobox input has `role=\"combobox\"` and `aria-expanded`, which provides some context, but:\n- The popup has **no role** \u2014 screen readers won't announce it as a list of options when it appears\n- Options have **no roles** \u2014 each suggestion isn't identified as an option\n- There's **no aria-activedescendant** \u2014 as the user presses ArrowDown through suggestions, nothing is announced about which item is highlighted. The user has zero feedback until they press Enter and the input value changes\n- There's **no aria-controls** \u2014 screen readers can't establish that the popup belongs to this combobox\n- When the list opens, there's no announcement like \"Suggestions list, 5 items\" or \"Option 1 of 5 selected\"\n\nA screen reader user would experience: type in field \u2192 see visual dropdown appear (but hear nothing about it) \u2192 press ArrowDown several times (hear nothing change) \u2192 press Enter hoping to select the right item. This is effectively a blind selection process.\n\n### Keyboard-only User (MEDIUM alarm)\nThe core keyboard interaction works \u2014 Tab reaches the input, arrow keys filter and navigate internally, Enter selects, Escape closes. However:\n- Options themselves are not focusable elements (bare divs with onClick only). If focus lands on an option through any means, Space/Enter won't activate it\n- There's no way to use screen reader reading mode or quick-navigation shortcuts to jump into the list and select an item directly \u2014 the user must rely entirely on the input's arrow key handling\n\n### Low Vision User (LOW alarm)\nStandard UI concerns apply but aren't specific to this component's a11y design. The native `<input>` element provides a visible focus indicator at 200% zoom. Visual \"selected\" class provides feedback for users who can see the screen. No specific low-vision barriers in the code itself (though visual contrast and touch target sizing would need separate evaluation).\n\n### Cognitive Accessibility (LOW alarm)\nThe interaction pattern is simple enough that cognitive demands are minimal. The main concern: without AT announcements, a user relying on screen readers might become confused about why they're pressing arrow keys with no feedback \u2014 this could cause abandonment or errors. For sighted users, the visual feedback is clear and consistent.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\nThe combobox component implements only ~40% of the WAI-ARIA APG Combobox pattern. While it has the basic `role=\"combobox\"`, `aria-autocomplete`, and `aria-expanded` attributes, it is missing the critical ARIA roles (`listbox`, `option`) that make the suggestion mechanism perceivable to screen readers at all. More fundamentally, it lacks any focus management strategy for assistive technology \u2014 no `aria-activedescendant` means highlighted option state is never communicated during arrow navigation, making selection effectively blind for screen reader users.\n\nThe component would fail real-world use by screen reader users: they type in the field, see a visual dropdown (but hear nothing about it), press ArrowDown through suggestions with zero feedback, and must guess which item will be selected when they press Enter. This is not an edge case \u2014 it's the primary interaction mode for comboboxes.\n\n**What would need to change for ACCEPT:**\n1. Add `role=\"listbox\"` to popup container (CRITICAL)\n2. Add `role=\"option\"` to each suggestion item (CRITICAL)  \n3. Implement `aria-activedescendant` on input pointing to option IDs, with unique IDs added to options (CRITICAL \u2014 the core gap)\n4. Add `aria-controls` linking input to popup (MAJOR)\n5. Add `aria-selected` to selected/highlighted option (MAJOR)\n\nThese changes would bring the component into compliance with the [WAI-ARIA APG Combobox pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/combobox/), specifically the \"Combobox with Listbox\" variant, which is exactly what this component attempts to implement.",
